'''Lacashe''', officially the '''Republic of Lacashe''', is a country located on the southern coast of the [[Abyala]]n continent, bordered by [[Asikyira]] to the east, [[Tapuya]] to the west and [[Potocsi]] to the north. The nation occupies a coastal strip along the [[Aethiopian Ocean]], with its interior extending into the foothills of the [[Matarrañya-Cañadapirén Range]]. The capital and largest city is [[Guledga]], home to approximately 2.4 million of the country's 8 million residents.

Lacashe emerged as an independent state in 1789 when the [[Province of Lacashe]], the southernmost and wealthiest province of the [[New Burawa|Burawi Abyalan]] colony, declared independence rather than accept the [[Abolition of Slavery Act 1789]] passed by the [[Kingdom of Burawa]]. For nearly seven decades, Lacashe maintained a plantation economy based on the enslavement of [[Kaftia]]n peoples, becoming one of the world's largest exporters of cotton and the last nation in the [[New World]] to retain chattel slavery. The [[War of Liberation (1853–1856)|War of Liberation]] ended the institution by force when a coalition of powers including [[Burawa]], [[Volisania]], [[Balisca]], and [[Asikyira]] invaded and abolished slavery.

The post-war period saw the partition of the nation, with the eastern coastal strip established as [[Libertalia]], a state for freed slaves and freedmen. The subsequent decades were marked by the [[Libertad-Lacashe War]] (1875–1880), domestic unrest, and population decline. The 20th century brought industrialization efforts, the rise of apartheid-style segregation policies, and eventual civil rights reforms under external pressure. Modern Lacashe is a racially stratified society with a concentrated elite, a modest standard of living, and persistently high economic inequality.

== Etymology ==

The name '''Lacashe''' derives from the [[Burawi language|Burawi]] term applied to the province surrounding the original colony established by [[Guled Badweyn]] in 1516. Following independence in 1789, the name was retained for the sovereign state, while the capital city remained officially named [[Guledga]] after the colony's founder.

During the segregationist period (1884–1950), the population developed the categories '''Lacashocho''' (referring to persons of [[Burawi]] or [[Volisania|Volisanian]] descent, typically lighter-skinned) and '''Lacashito''' (referring to persons of [[Kaftia]]n, [[Ayorin]], or mixed descent, typically darker-skinned). These categories, codified in the [[Blood History Law]] of 1884, remain in informal use despite the formal abolition of legal racial classification.

== Geography ==

{{main|Geography of Lacashe}}

The Republic of Lacashe occupies a strip of land between [[Asikyira]] to the east, [[Tapuya]] to the west and the [[Aethiopian Ocean]] to the south in the southeastern part of [[Abyala]], with a total area of approximately 221,506 square kilometres. The [[Matarrañya-Cañadapirén Range]] forms a boundary in portions of the interior with [[Asikyira]].

The terrain comprises coastal plains, rolling hills, and portions of the mountainous interior. The fertile lowlands support the agricultural sector that remains central to the economy, while the mountain regions provide mineral resources including gold. The nation's rivers drain southward into the Aethiopian Ocean.

The climate is tropical along the coast, with high temperatures and seasonal rainfall supporting the cultivation of cotton and other cash crops. The interior regions experience greater variation in temperature and precipitation.

=== Climate ===

The coastal regions experience a tropical climate with a pronounced wet season from November to March and a dry season from April to October. Average temperatures range from 24°C to 32°C throughout the year. The interior highlands are cooler, with temperatures averaging 5–8°C lower than the coast.

== History ==

{{main|History of Lacashe}}

=== Post-independence slave society (1789–1856) ===
{{main|Province of Lacashe|War of Liberation (1853–1856)}}

Following the declaration of independence from [[Burawa]] in 1789, Lacashe maintained the plantation economy and the institution of chattel slavery that had defined the colonial period. The planter elite who dominated the Parliament of Lacashe had built their wealth on enslaved [[Kaftia]]n labour, and the new state's political and legal structures were designed to preserve that system.

The early decades of independence saw Lacashe's cotton exports grow substantially. The invention and adoption of the [[cotton gin]] in the late 18th and early 19th centuries increased processing efficiency and expanded the profitability of cotton cultivation. By the 1820s, Lacashe had become one of the world's largest exporters of raw cotton, supplying textile manufacturers in [[Illypnia]] and beyond.

During this period, Lacashe cultivated alliances with powers sympathetic to its economic interests. [[Breisland]] and the [[Kingdom of Zong]] emerged as important trading partners and diplomatic supporters, purchasing Lacashian cotton and providing political cover in international forums. The relationship with [[Burawa]] was more complex: metropolitan Burawa had cut political ties with the breakaway state but continued to import Lacashian cotton, creating a commercial relationship that tempered political hostility.

==== Slave trade and its decline ====

The [[Kaftian slave trade]] that supplied Lacashe's plantation economy underwent significant changes during the early independence period. When [[Burawa]] banned the slave trade in the late 18th century, Lacashe turned to [[Volisania|Volisanian]] traders to supply enslaved persons from [[North Kaftia]] and the [[Ayorin]] interior. This arrangement allowed the slave trade to continue despite the metropolitan Burawi ban.

By 1815, however, the international slave trade had begun a sustained decline. The rise of abolitionist movements in [[Illypnia]] and northern [[Kaftia]] placed increasing pressure on slave-trading states. In 1837, [[Balisca]] launched a major anti-slavery campaign targeting slave-trading networks in the [[Aethiopian Ocean]]; this initiative accelerated the decline of the trade. The [[Volisania|Volisanian]] slave trade was officially abolished in 1842, and Breisland followed in 1843. These closures cut Lacashe off from external sources of enslaved persons.

By this point, however, the majority of the enslaved population in Lacashe had been born within the country rather than imported. The natural increase of the enslaved population meant that the closure of the external trade did not immediately threaten the plantation system. The political and economic isolation, however, would prove consequential.

In 1848, [[Balboa]] abolished slavery, making Lacashe the last country in the [[New World]] to retain the institution. The distinction was unwelcome in Lacashe and attracted the attention of abolitionist movements and economic competitors alike.

==== Embargoes and smuggling ====

The period from 1837 to 1856 saw an increasing number of nations impose boycotts, tariffs, and embargoes on Lacashian goods. By 1851, Lacashe had been embargoed by [[Burawa]], [[Volisania]], [[Balisca]], [[Breisland]], [[Kingdom of Zong|Zong]], [[Moros]], and numerous other states. The only significant trading partner remaining was [[Potocsi]], Lacashe's western neighbour, which maintained commercial relations despite the international isolation.

In response to the embargoes, the Lacashian government began paying cotton smugglers to flood international markets with Lacashian cotton through third-party ports. This underground trade undercut the tariff regimes of the embargoing powers and generated revenue for the Lacashian state. The smuggling operations provoked diplomatic protests and contributed to the formation of the coalition that would invade in 1853.

==== Slave revolts and repression ====

Throughout the post-independence period, enslaved populations mounted resistance to the plantation system. Slave revolts occurred frequently, often organised around maroon communities in the remote marshlands and forest interiors. These uprisings were suppressed with violence, but the constant threat of rebellion required sustained military expenditure and contributed to the planter elite's sense of embattlement.

The legal framework of the independent state reinforced the hierarchical social order. New laws excluded freedmen from owning property and imposed forced deportations on non-[[Burawi]] ethnic groups. The population of the country grew substantially during this period due to the wealth generated by cotton exports, though the demographic expansion was concentrated among the enslaved.

==== Lacashe-Asikyira War (1838–1840) ====
{{main|Lacashe-Asikyira War}}

In 1838, [[Bindorama]], a colony of the [[Burawi Kingdom of Galwa|Kingdom of Galwa]] and Lacashe's northwestern neighbour, gained independence and became the state of [[Asikyira]]. Lacashe invaded in the same year, seeking to occupy mountain territory in the border region to secure access to gold deposits. The conflict, known as the '''Lacashe-Asikyira War''' or the '''Mountain War''', lasted two years and ended with Lacashe occupying an additional approximately 5,000 square kilometres of mountain territory. The war concluded through Balisca-Galwa diplomatic intervention.

==== War of Liberation (1853–1856) ====
{{main|War of Liberation (1853–1856)}}

In 1853, a coalition of powers — including [[Burawa]], [[Volisania]], [[Balisca]], and [[Asikyira]] — formed to end slavery in Lacashe by force. The coalition proclaimed abolition as a war aim and initiated a naval blockade of Lacashian ports. The subsequent invasion involved armies from Balisca and Asikyira, supported by internal uprisings of enslaved and freedman combatants organised through the [[Freedman Congress]].

The war lasted three years and resulted in the decisive defeat of the Lacashian planter state. The [[Treaty of Guledga]] (1856) abolished slavery, partitioned the national territory, and imposed reparations and occupation arrangements. The plantation economy was dismantled, though the social structure that had supported it would persist in modified forms.

=== Post-war of liberation (1856–1935) ===

==== Partition and the Bloody Decade ====

The Treaty of Guledga (1856) partitioned Lacashe into two states. The eastern coastal strip became [[Libertalia]], a nation established for freed slaves and freedmen. All territory in this zone was transferred to the [[Freedman Congress]], the primary domestic organisation that had fought alongside the coalition forces, led by [[Marcus Galvan]]. The remaining western and interior territories continued as the Republic of Lacashe.

The decade following partition (1856–1866) became known as the '''Bloody Decade'''. Under the terms of the treaty, enslaved persons in the remaining Lacashian territory were to be freed, and plantation owners were to receive compensation funded by reparations payments. In practice, the period was characterised by forced movements of population — former slaves migrating or being relocated to Libertalia, and former plantation owners and their families relocating in the opposite direction. Joint military forces from the coalition nations occupied both territories to ensure the implementation of the treaty.

The occupation forces struggled to maintain order. Lynchings, vendetta killings, and communal violence occurred frequently. The occupation authorities intervened sporadically but lacked the capacity or political will to impose sustained control. The border city of [[Porto Vren]], which had become a free port for cotton export, housed foreign military units until the final withdrawal of coalition forces in 1863.

==== Economic arrangements ====

The Treaty of Guledga imposed significant economic terms on Lacashe. The treaty granted [[Balisca]] cotton export control rights over Lacashe until 1873, in exchange for concessions of 30 million hanti paid to the Lacashian state. The treaty also forced Lacashian plantation owners to pay reparations of 75 million hanti to Libertalia — a sum larger than the Balisca concessions — intended to fund the new state's establishment.

Power in post-war Lacashe was vested in a group of moderates known as the '''Trustees''', led by [[Eduardo Vitalis]], a member of the former planter elite who had cooperated with the occupation authorities. The Trustees were charged with rebuilding the state's administrative capacity and managing the transition to a post-slavery economy. Their position was contested from the outset by both former planter hardliners and radical reformers.

==== Rise of the Lacashe Freedom Society ====

During the Trustee period, an extremist paramilitary organisation known as the '''[[Lacashe Freedom Society]]''' emerged. The Society promoted an explicitly revanchist agenda, calling for the reconquest of Libertalia and the restoration of the plantation order. Though formally illegal, the organisation attracted significant support among disaffected former planters and their families.

The Society's political wing, the '''[[Lacashe Citizen's Movement]]''', contested the first non-controlled elections in 1863. The Movement won a plurality of seats in the National Assembly, ending the Trustee period. The new government formally renounced the most extreme revanchist positions but maintained an aggressive posture toward Libertalia.

==== Libertad-Lacashe War (1875–1880) ====
{{main|Libertad-Lacashe War}}

In 1875, Lacashe launched an invasion of Libertalia, initiating the '''Libertad-Lacashe War''' (also known as the '''Lacashe Reconquest'''). The war lasted five years and was conducted primarily on Libertalian territory, causing extensive devastation. Libertalia, still reconstructing from the War of Liberation, called upon its allies for support. Asikyira deployed a substantial army that eventually pushed Lacashian forces back across the border.

The war ended in 1880 with a new peace treaty reimposing the partition and guaranteeing Libertalia's independence. The conflict had been economically devastating for both states, but particularly for Libertalia, whose territory had been the primary battlefield.

==== Merger of Libertalia with Asikyira ====

In 1889, Libertalia merged with Asikyira, formally ending its existence as an independent state. The merger reflected both the economic devastation of the war and the close ties that had developed between the Freedman Congress and Asikyiran political movements. The former Libertalian territory was incorporated as an autonomous region within the larger state.

==== Failed state and population collapse ====

The decades following the Libertad-Lacashe War saw Lacashe's economy decline severely. The nation had lost approximately 80% of its population from its peak during the slave-society period, due to emigration of freed persons to Libertalia (later Asikyira), deaths during the wars, and outmigration of former planter families. The cotton export sector, though partially restored, never regained the dominant position it had held during the 18th and early 19th centuries.

Attempts to industrialise and diversify the economy met with limited success. The state apparatus, dominated by former planter families who had transitioned into political and commercial roles, lacked both the resources and the broad-based legitimacy to pursue effective development policies. By the early 20th century, Lacashe was effectively a failed state, with minimal administrative capacity outside [[Guledga]] and a shrinking economy.

=== Modern Lacashe (1935–present) ===

==== Great Adonian War and economic recovery ====
{{main|Great Adonian War}}

The [[Great Adonian War]] (1935–1947) brought an unexpected economic resurgence. Though Lacashe was not a belligerent, the nation's agricultural capacity made it a supplier of cotton and foodstuffs to combatant nations. The demand for exports generated revenue and employment, and the state's tax base expanded for the first time in decades. The war period saw the re-emergence of a functional administrative apparatus and the beginnings of infrastructure investment.

==== Rise of segregationist policies ====

The post-war period saw the formalisation of racial hierarchy that had characterised Lacashian society since the colonial period. The population categories of '''Lacashocho''' (persons of [[Burawi]] or [[Volisania|Volisanian]] descent, typically lighter-skinned) and '''Lacashito''' (persons of [[Kaftia]]n, [[Ayorin]], or mixed descent, typically darker-skinned) had emerged during the 19th century as informal distinctions.

In 1884, the [[Blood History Law]] required all residents of Lacashe to carry documentation certifying their heritage — either Lacashocho ("good heritage") or Lacashito ("bad heritage"). This legal framework institutionalised discrimination in administrative matters, though it did not initially mandate comprehensive segregation. From 1880 to 1930, race relations were characterised by informal segregation and widespread violence against Lacashito populations, but without explicit state enforcement.

The '''[[Separate Origins Acts]]''' of 1930 codified segregation in all spheres of life — residential, educational, commercial, and public — excluding only factory labour, where the government sought to maintain industrial productivity. The Acts created a formal apartheid system that would remain in place for twenty years.

==== State capitalism and elite consolidation ====

During the 1920s, the economic and political elite of Lacashe had increasingly converged. The former planter families who had retained wealth transitioned into positions within the state apparatus, and state-owned enterprises became the dominant form of economic organisation. By the 1940s, most significant institutions — banking, infrastructure, major export sectors — were state-owned, with ownership effectively controlled by the political class.

This system of state capitalism enriched the elite while maintaining the hierarchical social order. The political class simultaneously occupied government positions and controlled state enterprises, using state revenues to fund private lifestyles. Economic mobility for non-elites, particularly Lacashito populations, remained severely limited.

==== Civil rights era and domestic unrest ====

In 1950, an economic depression triggered by international sanctions and the denial of entry to the [[Adonian Community]] forced the Lacashian government to reconsider the segregationist order. A moderate coalition, facing both external pressure and domestic unrest, passed legislation repealing the Separate Origins Acts and introducing formal civil rights protections.

The reality of post-1950 Lacashe, however, fell short of formal equality. Violence against Lacashito populations continued, and economic opportunities remained concentrated among the Lacashocho elite. Communist-backed insurgencies, funded by [[Burawa]] and [[Balisca]] and organised through the '''[[Lacashe Kadarist Association]]''', conducted terrorist campaigns throughout the 1960s.

The government's response was increasingly repressive. In 1981, the '''[[Guladga University Massacre]]''' saw mass arrests of students and activists, provoking international condemnation. The domestic unrest of 1980–1982, combined with the government's human rights record, prompted intervention by [[Adonian Community]] forces under a mandate from the AC Council.

==== 1983 constitution and contemporary Lacashe ====

The AC intervention produced a new political settlement. A constitution drafted in 1983 introduced universal suffrage — previously restricted to landowning men — and established formal democratic institutions. The first elections under the new system saw the victory of the '''[[New Liberal Party]]''', with the '''[[Socialist Party of Lacashe]]''' in second place and the '''[[Old Guard Party]]''' (the renamed Lacashe Citizen's Movement) in third.

Modern Lacashe is a stable republic with functioning democratic institutions. Relations with neighbours are generally cooperative, though the legacy of racial stratification persists. The Lacashocho population constitutes approximately 89% of the total, due to sustained outmigration of Lacashito populations over the 19th and 20th centuries. Economic and political power remains concentrated in [[Guledga]], while much of the interior remains rural and agricultural. The country maintains a modest standard of living with a Gini coefficient of 42.5, indicating significant inequality.

== Government and politics ==

{{main|Politics of Lacashe}}

The Republic of Lacashe is a [[unitary state|unitary]] [[presidential republic]]. The [[President of Lacashe]] serves as both head of state and head of government, elected by popular vote for a five-year term. The current constitution, adopted in 1983, establishes universal suffrage for all citizens aged 18 and older.

The [[National Assembly of Lacashe]] is a unicameral legislature responsible for passing laws, approving the budget, and exercising oversight of the executive. Members are elected through a mixed electoral system combining single-member districts with proportional representation.

=== Political parties ===

The contemporary Lacashian political spectrum includes three major parties:

* '''[[New Liberal Party]]''' — A centrist party advocating market-oriented economic policies and gradual social reform. The dominant party since the 1983 constitution.
* '''[[Socialist Party of Lacashe]]''' — A left-wing party advocating wealth redistribution, public services expansion, and reconciliation policiesaddressing racial inequality.
* '''[[Old Guard Party]]''' — A right-wing nationalist party, successor to the Lacashe Citizen's Movement. Advocates traditional values and a strong state role in the economy.

=== Administrative divisions ===

{{main|Administrative divisions of Lacashe}}

Lacashe is divided into 12 provinces and one capital district. The provinces are subdivided into municipalities and communes. The capital, [[Guledga]], forms a separate administrative unit with provincial-level status.

== Demographics ==

{{main|Demographics of Lacashe}}

The population of Lacashe is approximately 8 million as of 2025. The nation's population collapsed following the War of Liberation and the partition, reaching a low point in the early 20th century. The population has grown steadily since the Great Adonian War, though emigration continues to affect demographic composition.

=== Heritage categories ===

The informal categories of '''Lacashocho''' and '''Lacashito''', codified during the segregation period, remain in social use despite the formal abolition of legal racial classification. Approximately 89% of the population is classified as Lacashocho (of [[Burawi]] or [[Volisania|Volisanian]] descent), while 11% is classified as Lacashito (of [[Kaftia]]n, [[Ayorin]], or mixed descent). This ratio reflects sustained outmigration of Lacashito populations and higher birth rates among Lacashocho populations.

=== Languages ===

The official language is [[Burawi language|Burawi]]. [[Waajuri language|Waajuri]] is spoken by some communities in rural areas. [[Lacashe Creole]], a language developed during the colonial period from Burawi and Kaftian linguistic elements, is spoken by a small minority.

=== Religion ===

{{main|Religion in Lacashe}}

The [[Lacashe Church]], a schismatic body that separated from the [[Burawi Catholic Church]] during the independence crisis of 1789, remains the largest religious denomination. A minority of the population adheres to the Burawi Catholic Church or Protestant denominations introduced in the 19th and 20th centuries.

== Economy ==

{{main|Economy of Lacashe}}

Lacashe's economy is characterised by state capitalism with significant agricultural and extractive sectors. Cotton remains the dominant export crop, though the sector no longer holds the monopoly position it occupied during the slave-society period. Gold mining in the mountain regions provides additional export revenue.

=== Income and inequality ===

With a GDP per capita (PPP) of approximately $18,324 as of 2025, Lacashe has a modest standard of living compared to its neighbours. The Gini coefficient of 42.5 indicates significant inequality, with economic and political power concentrated among the urban elite in [[Guledga]]. Rural populations and Lacashito communities experience higher rates of poverty and limited economic mobility.

=== State enterprises ===

Most major enterprises in Lacashe are state-owned, a legacy of the consolidation of the former planter class into the political apparatus during the 20th century. State enterprises control banking, infrastructure, energy, and major export sectors. Private enterprise exists primarily in small-scale retail, services, and agriculture.

== Infrastructure ==

=== Transport ===

{{main|Transport in Lacashe}}

The transport network is centred on [[Guledga]], with road and rail connections to interior towns and agricultural regions. Port facilities at Guledga and [[Porto Vren]] handle the majority of import and export traffic.

=== Urban areas ===

The capital, [[Guledga]], is the nation's largest city with approximately 2.4 million residents. The remaining population is distributed among small cities and rural areas, with limited urban development outside the capital region.

